    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • The Volkswagen Group's Affordable BEV Quartet Has Almost Reached 150,000 Orders\nChinese Regulators Propose New Car Design Rules That Make a Whole Lot of Sense\n19 photos\nPhoto: Xiaomi\nEver had a moment when you needed the wipers or demister, and the only way to turn either of them on was to use touch controls? Chinese regulators have had enough of that distracting nonsense. A new draft published by the Ministry of Industry and Information Technology would force automakers to keep physical controls for many vital functions, ban folding and flexible displays, and make them prove new designs can actually survive a year of real-world abuse.\nPublished on the MIIT's website on October 10, comments on the new draft are open through November 10. Per said proposal, displays would face assessments for extreme weather reliability, crash safety, and - most importantly - distraction. China is also planning to ban flexible or foldable in-vehicle displays, which are completely nonsensical when you think about it from a practical standpoint.\nThe physical controls requirement is the part resonating most with me and pretty much everyone who understands what muscle memory stands for in this context. Lights, wipers, and demisting/defrosting controls have to be physical under this proposal. Touch- and pressure-sensitive buttons would face checks for accidental operation and clear feedback.\nThen there are emergency escape controls, emergency braking controls, and the drive system's power cut-off, which are required to embrace physical hardware with conspicuous markings to boot. So far, the Chinese regulators are as sensible as you would expect them to be.\nRegulators are targeting voice control as well. If the vehicle is being driven, passengers would not be allowed to directly operate the headlights or any other vital function through voice control. Gesture control and similar interfaces are targeted as well. The wording of the draft clearly targets non-driving occupants.\nPhoto: Xiaomi Carmakers would also have to assess recognition, command confirmation, and protection against unintended operation. The seating provisions would keep zero-gravity and similar automotive seat designs from reclining, swiveling, and everything in between while driving. Door handles receive an explicit prohibition of completely hidden designs, alongside requirements for mechanical redundancy and sufficient hand-operating space.\nSoft-close doors would need anti-pinch protection and manual opening after a malfunction or power loss. The Chinese draft further poses the question of what happens after mud or water obstructs rearview cameras that feed into camera-only mirrors. In other words, the Polestar 4 and similar vehicles are targeted by this very sensible question. That's not all, though.\nIntegrated lamps, protruding lights, and bumpers that hide fragile componentry like air suspension reservoirs would require repair cost assessments. Combining functions might be impressive from an engineering standpoint, but the proposed review is focused on material reliability and how that complexity affects repair costs. Exterior message displays would need content oversight as well.\nAnother burden that would affect carmakers is passenger car reliability validation, which is planned to cover at least 30,000 kilometers (18,641 miles) across varied conditions. Environmental testing would last at least one year, and software would need real-world validation as well.\nExisting approved models with relevant designs would need supplementary evidence before July 1, 2027, which means that automakers operating in the Chinese market have very little time to make the required changes.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nChina industry MIIT safety design\nDownload: China's October 2026 Technical Guidelines for Automotive Product Innovative Design (PDF)\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT Not an Optical Illusion: Four Differently Sized Bentley Blowers Come Together for the First Time",
